The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
public static synchronized ObjectSelector getInstance() {
    if (myInstance == null)
        myInstance = new ObjectSelector();

    myInstance.treeView.getHorizontalScrollBar().getModel().setValue(0);
}
```
Reason:
The horizontal scroll bar is reset to 0 every time the `getInstance` method is called, which can happen frequently during the operation of the model. This constant resetting causes the scroll bar to jitter, disrupting the user interface experience.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
@Override
public void setVisible(boolean bool) {
    if (bool && !isVisible()) {
        myInstance.treeView.getHorizontalScrollBar().getModel().setValue(0);
        myInstance.treeView.getVerticalScrollBar().getModel().setValue(0);
    }
    super.setVisible(bool);
}
```
Reason:
The reset of the horizontal scroll bar is now moved to the `setVisible` method, which is only called when the visibility of the `ObjectSelector` changes. This prevents the scroll bar from being reset during frequent calls to `getInstance`, thus eliminating the jittering effect while the model is running.

